  "why": "Project rule (step 15 round 3): a call on an object a lower-case callee made under a built-in's name (`disp(g)` after `g = gadget(…)`) keeps its row, dormant, while a class of the callee's name exists in any package and any case; else it is dropped. The construction is written three ways here: on an object of another class (`g = gadget(o)`, recorded `Holder::gadget`, a failed row), through an import (`import qpk.*`, linked to `+qpk/gadget.m`), and, in a script, as `g = gadget(:)`, which the extraction reads as an index into an array (project rule: `x(:)` with no variable `x` is array evidence), no call and no object of any class. With a root `disp.m` the call is the plain one, linked to it (as `disp(g)` with no class is); with a `disp.m` in another folder's `private/`, out of the caller's reach, the row is kept dormant without a class too (the rule's second clause), and a class arriving then keeps that one row. A sync that brings a class `Gadget` and takes it away, with and without `disp.m`, must end where a fresh index ends.",
